<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph de Maistre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1753–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Joseph Marie, comte de Maistre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph de Maistre was a Savoyard lawyer, diplomat and political philosopher, remembered as one of the intellectual forefathers of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a>. Born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a>, a subject of the King of Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, he was at first a liberal, but turned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> after the August Decrees of 1789 and fled Chambéry when a French revolutionary army took it in 1792. As ambassador to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> he wrote his most influential works, and from 1817 he was minister of state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. In Considerations on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> (1796) he argued that the Revolution was divine punishment, and he defended monarchy and the ultimate authority of the Pope.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-de-maistre.jpg" alt="Joseph de Maistre" width="330" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Christian Vogel von Vogelstein · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jmaistre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Another form of conservatism grew in France from the Counter-Enlightenment works of <b>Joseph de Maistre</b> and Louis de Bonald, and most continental conservatives favoured close cooperation between state and Catholic Church.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
